## Baguette vs Emerald Cut: The Full Comparison

Both baguette and emerald cut belong to The Step-Cut Family. This is where their similarity ends and their differences begin.

<table class="dc-custom-table">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Factor</th>
      <th>Baguette</th>
      <th>Emerald Cut</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Facet count</td>
      <td>14</td>
      <td>57–58</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Typical use</td>
      <td>Accent / side stone / band</td>
      <td>Center stone</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>L:W ratio</td>
      <td>2:1 to 3:1</td>
      <td>1.30–1.50</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Depth</td>
      <td>Shallow (flat bottom)</td>
      <td>60–64% depth</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Brilliance</td>
      <td>Minimal — shows color clearly</td>
      <td>Hall-of-mirrors reflection</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Clarity requirement</td>
      <td>VS2+ (inclusions visible)</td>
      <td>VS2+ (inclusions visible)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Color sensitivity</td>
      <td>High — shows warmth readily</td>
      <td>High — shows warmth in step table</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Price per carat</td>
      <td>Lower — accent cut pricing</td>
      <td>Higher — center stone pricing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Setting use</td>
      <td>Channel bands, eternity, side stones</td>
      <td>Solitaire, pavé, halo, three-stone</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

The practical implication: when a buyer searches "baguette emerald cut diamond ring," they are most often looking for one of three things. A ring that has both cuts together (The Accent Advantage). A baguette band to pair with an emerald cut engagement ring.

Or a ring that has the clean, step-cut aesthetic of both cuts without necessarily containing both.

All three searches are satisfied by the 18 designs in this guide.

